# Rule: Static Validation on Edit

Static validation is the harness's job, not the agent's. A project that relies
on an agent *remembering* to lint has turned a deterministic check into a prompt
instruction — the first thing dropped when context fills up.

Install the hook via the `static-validation-hooks` skill.

## What it provides

One file, `.agents/hooks/validate-on-edit.sh`, holding two layers:

1. **Runner** — generic, copied as-is. Agent protocol (Claude `PostToolUse`,
   Cursor `afterFileEdit`), per-edit time budget, cached container resolution,
   retry brake, warn-once degradation.
2. **Routing table** — project-specific, between the `BEGIN/END ROUTING TABLE`
   markers. Written in five verbs: `svc`, `strip`, `fix`, `check`, `skip`.

Plus a thin registration file per agent host (`.claude/settings.json`,
`.cursor/hooks.json`). There is no cross-agent hook standard: the logic lives
once under `.agents/`, each host gets a pointer.

## When to install

- Any project with Docker and a dev stack the team already runs (`make up`).
- Any project where agents perform repeated edits.

## When NOT to install

- Projects with no container running during development — the hook resolves a
  live container and silently degrades without one.
- Languages whose only checker is whole-project (see below); there is nothing
  fast enough to put in the table.

## Non-negotiables

| Rule                                                    | Why                                                        |
| ------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| File-local linters only (eslint, biome, ruff, gofmt)    | The hook is synchronous on every write.                    |
| No `tsc --noEmit`, `phpstan`, `mypy`, no test runners   | Whole-project, 10-40s. They belong in CI and verify.       |
| Workspace branches before generic extension branches    | Otherwise the wrong workspace config lints the file.       |
| Empty `*)` catch-all, never `skip`                      | An unroutable type must warn once, not rot silently.       |
| Violations block (`exit 2`); everything else warns once | Unactionable noise trains agents to ignore hook output.    |

## Coverage limits — the verify phase stays mandatory

- Files written through the Bash tool (`cat >`, `sed -i`, code generators)
  never trigger the hook.
- In a linked git worktree the hook validates only if that worktree has its own
  stack; when `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` pins it to the main checkout's stack it
  skips, because linting the main checkout's stale copy is a false pass.

